    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d850">

                <pb facs="0832" n="734" sameAs="frus:frus1930v02/pg734"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">836.48 1930 Hurricane/40: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Minister in the Dominican Republic</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Curtis</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-09-10T12:00:00-04:00">September 10,
                        1930—noon</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>48. Your September 9, 11 a.m.,<anchor n="19" xml:id="d850-f1"/> and September 9,
                    noon. The Department is most reluctant to have a marine detachment sent to Santo
                    Domingo and will consent only if you consider their presence absolutely
                    necessary. In any event the Department would have to have a formal definite
                    request from the Dominican Government to you. If you are sure that it is
                    necessary you may explain to the President that this Government is most anxious
                    to help him but <pb facs="0833" n="735" xml:id="pg735"/> that the sending of
                    marines will create misunderstanding in other countries. Even if you want them
                    we may not agree to send them.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Cotton</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
